WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.790
Press Run once, and the cat
and the dog both start moving.

2
00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:13.026
Nobody waits for anybody. Two
algorithms, running at the same time.

3
00:00:13.526 --> 00:00:17.287
Two objects are standing on
the stage: a cat and a dog.

4
00:00:17.507 --> 00:00:22.058
Each object has its own script.
The cat moves right, then jumps.

5
00:00:22.278 --> 00:00:26.458
The dog's script is different.
It jumps, and then jumps again.

6
00:00:26.678 --> 00:00:31.368
Press Run. In beat one the cat
moves right while the dog jumps.

7
00:00:31.588 --> 00:00:35.257
In beat two they both jump. The
dog never waits for the cat.

8
00:00:35.757 --> 00:00:39.519
Every object keeps its own
script, on a tab of its own.

9
00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:43.268
Tap the cat's tab, and build
the cat's two blocks there.

10
00:00:43.488 --> 00:00:47.111
Tap the dog's tab, and build
the dog's two blocks too.

11
00:00:47.331 --> 00:00:50.442
Now press Run, and both
scripts go at once.

12
00:00:50.662 --> 00:00:54.563
Build both scripts on the cat,
and the dog does nothing at all.

13
00:00:54.783 --> 00:00:58.359
So check the tab first. Whose
program are you building?

14
00:00:58.859 --> 00:01:02.156
Now a tree joins the cat
and the dog on the stage.

15
00:01:02.376 --> 00:01:06.184
A tree is static. Static
means it stays where it is.

16
00:01:06.404 --> 00:01:09.748
A static object can still
have a script of its own.

17
00:01:09.968 --> 00:01:13.637
The tree says hello and
grows bigger. It never moves.

18
00:01:13.857 --> 00:01:17.804
Its palette has no move blocks.
That is what static means.

19
00:01:18.024 --> 00:01:21.832
All three run at once: two
movers and one static object.

20
00:01:22.332 --> 00:01:26.512
Programmers often work in pairs,
and it brings four benefits.

21
00:01:26.732 --> 00:01:31.097
Two people have more ideas than
one person working on their own.

22
00:01:31.317 --> 00:01:36.054
Your partner is a second pair of
eyes, and spots the bug you missed.

23
00:01:36.274 --> 00:01:40.314
Share the work: you build the
cat, your partner builds the dog.

24
00:01:40.534 --> 00:01:44.203
Say your plan out loud, and it
gets clearer in your own head.

25
00:01:44.423 --> 00:01:49.856
Asking for help does not mean you cannot
do it. Real programmers ask all the time.

26
00:01:50.356 --> 00:01:53.700
Every programmer makes mistakes,
and good ones use them.

27
00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:57.496
The cat started in the wrong
place. Nothing had reset it.

28
00:01:57.716 --> 00:02:01.385
Now you know: reset first.
A go home block at the top.

29
00:02:01.605 --> 00:02:06.016
The repeat repeated a spin, not
a jump. Check the block after it.

30
00:02:06.236 --> 00:02:09.859
Programmers write their
mistakes down in a notebook.

31
00:02:10.079 --> 00:02:14.583
Then the next program does not have
them. A mistake is information.

32
00:02:15.083 --> 00:02:19.356
More than one script can run
at the same time, from one Run.

33
00:02:19.576 --> 00:02:23.523
A static object stays where it
is, and it still has a script.

34
00:02:23.743 --> 00:02:27.040
Work with a partner, and you
get a second pair of eyes.

35
00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:30.836
And every mistake tells you
what to change next time.
